    "text": "A refrigerator keeps food cold by moving heat from inside to outside, like carrying warmth away in a bucket. A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. Inside the fridge, the refrigerant absorbs heat and turns into a gas. Then a compressor squeezes that gas, making it hot. Next, the hot gas travels through coils on the back or bottom of the fridge, where it releases heat into your kitchen and turns back into a liquid. Finally, it flows back inside to soak up more heat. Because heat keeps getting removed, the inside stays cold, slowing down the bacteria that make food spoil."
